  "preference_text": "\n\n--- Refinement ---\n[This is a design revision of the existing mock. Goal: Add a new Router example app to BWS4 demonstrating the routing pattern \u2014 a cheap classification call that decides which existing path handles a request, after which code dispatches and the model gets out of the way \u2014 in deliberate contrast with Tool Use, Planning Agent and ReAct Loop. Update the mock to reflect this revision's added features (Router_Example_App). Preserve the existing look and feel \u2014 colour scheme, typography, spacing, layout, and every screen or element these changes do not touch. Change only what these feature updates require.]\n\nAdd a Router Example App to the gallery, styled and laid out exactly like the existing example apps (same card chrome, header, run controls, history treatment). Do not change any other example or shared element.\n\nInput area. Six preset request buttons plus a free-text field. Presets are the primary input; free text is secondary. Each preset is labelled with its text only; the expected route is not shown on the button.\n\nRouter knowledge panel. A small, always-visible block showing the four one-line route descriptions byte-for-byte (Deterministic evaluator, Single call, RAG over the space dataset, Search + generate) and the decision margin as a number. This is literally what the router was told; label it that way.\n\nRun flow. A run reserves 2 of the visitor's 5 model-call units up front; show the units remaining. The run has exactly two ending states, each its own card:\n\nRouted answer card. A decision strip at the top: the four route scores as small bars or numbers, the chosen route highlighted, and the one-line rationale beneath. Below it, the answer, clearly labelled with the path that produced it. For the deterministic route, show the validated expression and its result. For RAG, show citations into the dataset. Two fallback variants of this same card: \"evaluator could not compute the expression; answered by single call instead\" and \"router decision was invalid; answered by single call instead.\" Both are plain one-line notes inside the card, not error dialogs.\nLow-confidence card. Shown when the top two scores are within the margin. Names both routes the router was torn between, states that the cheaper one was dispatched and why (fixed cost order), shows the same score strip, and then the answer from that route.\n\nCost strip at the bottom of every finished run: this run's cost by resource type (model calls, search calls, retrieval) versus the \"no router\" baseline (search + generate), with router overhead as a separate line item. Baseline latency is shown as the rolling median of real search-route runs, labelled \"typical\"; when fewer than 5 observations exist, show counts only and no time figure. Router call time is shown separately from the routed path's time.\n\nComparison re-run. When a preset was dispatched to a route other than its expected route, offer a \"Run it the expected way\" button on the card. It costs one run, shows router overhead as an explicit 0, and the result is displayed side by side with the original. Mark it as not feeding agreement history.\n\nAgreement panel. Per-preset agreement rate (agreed / (agreed + disagreed + declined)), shown only once a preset has 5 or more observations; below that, show the counts without a rate. A footnote: tie-break dispatches earn no credit.\n\nUse the same empty states, loading states and typography as the other example cards. Keep the Router card to the gallery's existing width; the decision strip and cost strip stack vertically rather than widening the card.\n\nShow the Router card with one completed run: preset 3, routed to RAG.\n\n--- Fix render errors ---\nFix these errors in the current mock without changing its design.\nKeep every screen, element and id; return the whole corrected document.\n- Uncaught SyntaxError: Missing initializer in const declaration (line 280)",
